The Karate Kid Part II is a 1986 American martial arts drama film
written by Robert Mark Kamen and directed by John G.

15
01:00:32,142 --> 01:00:38,142
It is the second installment in The Karate Kid franchise and the sequel to the
1984 film The Karate Kid, starring Ralph Macchio and Pat Morita.

16
01:04:34,285 --> 01:04:40,285
The Karate Kid Part II follows Daniel LaRusso (Macchio),
who accompanies his karate teacher Mr.

17
01:08:36,428 --> 01:08:42,428
Miyagi (Morita) to see his dying father in Okinawa, only to encounter an old
friend-turned-rival with a long-harbored grudge against Miyagi.

18
01:12:38,571 --> 01:12:44,571
Following the success of the first installment,
preparation for a sequel began immediately.

19
01:16:40,714 --> 01:16:46,714
Upon completion of the final script, Macchio and Morita were re-signed and
additional casting took place between May and July 1985.

20
01:20:42,857 --> 01:20:48,857
Principal photography began in September in Los
Angeles, and filming completed in December.

21
01:24:45,000 --> 01:24:51,000
Locations included Oahu, which was used to represent Okinawa in the film.

22
01:28:47,142 --> 01:28:53,142
The Karate Kid Part II was theatrically released in
the United States on June 20, 1986.

23
01:32:49,285 --> 01:32:55,285
The film received mixed reviews, with critics praising Morita's performance as well as the new location and
characters, while others criticized elements of the storyline, the antagonists, and some of the action scenes.

24
01:36:51,428 --> 01:36:57,428
The film was a commercial success, grossing $130 million worldwide (the same amount as
the first film), making it one of the highest-grossing films of 1986.

25
01:40:53,571 --> 01:40:59,571
A sequel titled The Karate Kid Part III was released in 1989.

26
01:44:55,714 --> 01:45:01,714
The film explores themes of underdog, martial
arts, japan, sports, death of father.

27
01:48:57,857 --> 01:49:03,857
The tone is emotional.
